<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Beautiful Mola</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> A Mola Has Many Colors!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Identify the setting and characters in audio storybooks.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can identify the setting and main characters in simple stories about molas (e.g., 'This story is about a girl making a mola.').<br>Can take part in exchanges about mola colors (e.g., 'What color is it?' 'It is red.').<br>Can respond to simple questions about molas (e.g., 'Is it pretty?' 'Yes, it is colorful.').</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays several large pictures of molas with vibrant colors and different animal designs (turtle, butterfly, jaguar). The teacher points to a mola and asks, "What do you see?" "What colors does it have?" Students point and say colors. The teacher introduces the word "mola" and writes it on the board. (Pragmatic: Describing things: 'A mola has many colors.')</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a mola picture and models the sentence "A mola has many vibrant colors." The teacher then points to specific elements, modeling "This mola has a red turtle and a blue butterfly." The teacher emphasizes the /m/ sound in 'mola' and the /k/ sound in 'colors' clearly. The teacher introduces vocabulary flashcards for 'turtle,' 'butterfly,' 'jaguar,' 'turquoise,' 'golden,' 'vibrant,' 'unique,' 'traditional.' (Pronunciation: sounds: /m/ in 'mola' and /k/ in 'colors').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a mola usually one color or many colors?" (Expected answer: "Many colors!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do molas have animals like butterflies?" (Expected answer: "Yes!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "The Beautiful Mola".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, simple audio storybook excerpt (2-3 minutes) titled "Ana's First Mola," featuring a girl making a mola. The story describes Ana choosing colors and designs (turtle, butterfly) and the process of cutting and sewing. The audio will use target vocabulary (mola, vibrant, turquoise, golden, turtle, butterfly, cut, sew, express, creativity) and grammar (Present simple: "A mola has...", 'First, cut...'). Visual aids (pictures representing key moments, characters, and objects) are displayed for support. (Genre: narrative excerpt, Register: informal, Format: audio story with accompanying visuals).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student identifies key information from short, simple audio messages or stories, such as the main characters and setting. (Listening: Can identify the setting and main characters in simple stories about molas).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Listening for gist):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher plays the audio story once. Students listen quietly and look at the pictures.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question 1:</b> "Who is the story about?" (Teacher pauses audio, elicits "Ana" or "a girl").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question 2:</b> "What is Ana making?" (Teacher elicits "a mola").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Matching Task:</b> The teacher holds up flashcards of a 'turtle' and a 'butterfly'. "Which animal did Ana choose for her mola?" Students listen again to a specific part of the audio and point to the correct flashcard.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice identifying specific details (colors, animals, actions) from the audio story. (Grammar: Present simple: 'A mola has...', 'because'. Vocabulary: mola, creativity, project, inspiration, culture, turtle, butterfly, jaguar, cut, sew, paste, choose, express, illustrate, decorate, collaborate, turquoise, golden, round, vibrant, traditional, unique, carefully, skillfully, artistically, also, and, because, first, second, third, next, last).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Listen and Circle):</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with pictures of molas and simple sentences with blanks. For example: "Ana's mola has a ______ turtle." or "She uses ______ fabric." Students listen to specific sentences from the audio story (played multiple times) and circle the correct word from a provided word bank (e.g., 'golden'/'silver'; 'red'/'turquoise'). (Phonemic Awareness: Spell one-syllable or two-syllable words related to colors and instructions: 'red,' 'orange,' 'vibrant,' 'second').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Substitution Drill (Listen and Point):</b> The teacher plays short phrases from the audio describing actions (e.g., "Ana likes to *cut* the fabric carefully." "She wants to *sew* the pieces together."). Students point to action pictures on their desks (e.g., a picture of cutting, a picture of sewing). The teacher can also ask, "What does Ana like to do? Cut or paste?" Students listen and indicate. (Pragmatic: Describing project steps: Using instructional language to describe creative tasks).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Listen and Confirm):</b> In pairs, students receive two different mola pictures (A and B). Student A describes their mola picture using simple sentences learned (e.g., "My mola has a red butterfly. It has many vibrant colors."). Student B listens and identifies which picture (A or B) Student A is describing. Then they switch roles. Students also practice expressing preferences: "I like this mola because it has bright colors." (Sociolinguistic: Participating in collaborative art-making: acknowledging peers' contributions and sharing feedback).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "The Beautiful Mola".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format (Listen and Create):</b> "Follow the Mola Instructions." Students work individually or in small groups. Each student receives a blank mola outline worksheet and a small bag of pre-cut paper shapes (round, squares, triangles) and paper animals (turtle, butterfly, jaguar) in various colors (turquoise, golden, red, yellow). The teacher gives a series of audio instructions (e.g., "First, choose a turquoise round shape and paste it on your mola. Second, choose a golden butterfly and paste it carefully. Third, make your mola vibrant with red colors."). Students listen and create their own mola collage by following the teacher's instructions. The focus is on listening carefully to the sequence and details. (Grammar: Instructional steps: 'First, cut the fabric...').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students also listen to a peer describe their mola after creation: "Your mola is very creative! I like your color choices because they are vibrant." (Pragmatic: Expressing preferences: 'I like this mola because it has many shapes.').</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Follow the Mola Instructions" activity, focusing on students' ability to follow multi-step audio instructions and identify key elements. </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> "Listen and Draw/Identify." The teacher plays a final short audio description of a mola (e.g., "This mola has a big jaguar. It is golden and unique. It has many round shapes."). Students listen and either draw the described mola or choose the correct mola picture from a set of three options. The teacher also asks three simple listening comprehension questions verbally: "What animal did you hear?" "What color is the animal?" "Is the mola traditional or modern?" (Learning Outcome: Can respond to simple questions about molas).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in identifying characters/elements from audio (e.g., drawing correct animal).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correctly following audio instructions (e.g., pasting correct shapes/colors).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Comprehension of simple questions about molas.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick "Mola Learning Check."</p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word I heard today was ______."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can listen for ______ (colors/animals) in a story." (Students check a box for what they feel confident identifying).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of the mola story was easy for you to understand?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What words about molas do you want to hear more next time?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson? (e.g., The visuals helped students understand the audio story; students enjoyed identifying the animals).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., Provide more pre-teaching of verbs like 'cut' and 'sew' with TPR; ensure clearer audio quality; use more varied question types for listening comprehension).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 2 (Reading Comprehension), students will read a short text about making a mola, encountering the same key vocabulary words and simple sentences in written form to reinforce their understanding from today's listening activities.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>